=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The NOC's report of a flapping OSPF area is a false premise. A thorough examination
of every OSPF-speaking device in Kath4 (router1, router7, router8, router10, router11,
router16, router17, mswitch0, sshswitch) shows the domain is fully converged and has
been stable since initial bring-up.

The decisive evidence is the OSPF LSDB sequence numbers. All ten Network LSAs in Area
0.0.0.0 carry sequence 0x80000001 — the first value assigned when an adjacency first
forms — across every router. This proves no adjacency has ever dropped since the lab
was started. LSA checksums are byte-for-byte identical on all routers (Router LSA sum
0x0004fbb0, Network LSA sum 0x0005008b), confirming a fully synchronised database.
The last SPF run on every device was more than three minutes ago, and SPF counts
(13–17) are consistent with a single clean initial convergence. No MaxAge LSAs, no
pending retransmits, and no empty retransmit/request queues were found.

The NOC's specific suspects — the router7↔router8 link (172.25.1.x, Net LSA
seq 0x80000001) and the mswitch0 uplink (10.2.2.x via router16's bond0, also
0x80000001) — are both perfectly stable. The bond0 round-robin mode on router16 and
router17 with MII polling disabled is a configuration concern for future resilience,
but it is not causing any current instability: both bonds report MII Status up with
zero link failure counts on all slave interfaces, and mswitch0/sshswitch correctly
bridge both slave ports into a single OSPF broadcast segment (br0 / br_v1).

Root cause: There is no fault. The OSPF area is stable and the flapping report is
unfounded.

Corrective action: No OSPF change is required. As a hardening measure, consider
configuring the bond0 interfaces on router16 and router17 with active-backup mode and
an MII polling interval (e.g. miimon=100) to enable proper link-failure detection.
=== END ===
```